`src/features/overview/sub_incidents/libs/incidentDeepLink.ts` is a 28-line module whose doc comment names the race exactly: engaging Athena's `incident_blocker` nudge navigates to Overview -> Incidents, which lazy-mounts `IncidentsInbox`, so `storeBus.emit('incidents:open-detail')` "can therefore land BEFORE the inbox subscribes — storeBus is fire-and-forget with no replay."

```ts
let _pendingIncidentId: string | null = null;
export function consumePendingIncidentDeepLink(): string | null {
  const id = _pendingIncidentId; _pendingIncidentId = null; return id;
}
```

The emitter calls `setPendingIncidentDeepLink(id)` *alongside* the emit. `IncidentsInbox.tsx` (mount effect) covers both timings: `const pending = consumePendingIncidentDeepLink(); if (pending) openById(pending);` then `storeBus.on('incidents:open-detail', ...)`. Single-slot by design — only the latest deep-link matters — and consuming clears it so a manual revisit to the tab does not re-pop the modal. Refines the existing claim-and-clear one-shot-slot canon for the lazy-mount race specifically.

`src/features/overview/sub_incidents/components/IncidentsInbox.tsx` — `loadPersistedFilters()`:

```ts
// `since` is an absolute timestamp that would go stale between sessions, and
// `persona_id` is a transient detail-modal drill-in — both reset to null on load
return {
  statuses: saved.statuses ?? DEFAULT_FILTERS.statuses,
  severities: saved.severities ?? null,
  source_tables: saved.source_tables ?? null,
  persona_id: null,
  since: null,
};
```

The write side is symmetric: the persistence effect destructures only `{ statuses, severities, source_tables }` before `localStorage.setItem(FILTERS_KEY, ...)`. The companion module `libs/incidentFilterDefaults.ts` keeps the resting state (`OPEN_ONLY_FILTERS`) and the `isNarrowedFilters()` predicate in one shared module used by the inbox, the filter bar's clear target, and the KPI header "so the three views can never silently diverge."

`src/features/overview/sub_incidents/components/IncidentsInbox.tsx`:

```ts
// Armed by an actual incident action (ack / resolve / dismiss) so a filter
// change that yields zero never triggers the celebration — only clearing the
// open inbox does.
const clearedByActionRef = useRef(false);
```

The judgment runs in an effect that waits for `loading` to settle: rows present -> reset both flag and ref; zero rows AND `clearedByActionRef.current` AND `!isNarrowed` -> `setJustCleared(true)`; the ref is disarmed either way. The `InboxZero` empty state receives `celebrate={justCleared}` only on the default open-only view — a zero on a narrowed filter renders a plain "no match" `EmptyState` instead. Composes three signals (who caused zero, whether the fetch finished, whether the view is the resting one) that a naive `items.length === 0 && confetti()` conflates.

`src/features/overview/sub_memories/components/MemoryConflictReview.tsx` — `handleResolve`:

```ts
// Resolve to explicit keep/delete memories and ASSERT they differ, so
// any future reorder of memoryA/memoryB (the `superseded` kind already
// swaps them) can never silently hard-delete the one the user chose to keep.
const keep = resolution === 'keep_a' ? conflict.memoryA : conflict.memoryB;
const remove = resolution === 'keep_a' ? conflict.memoryB : conflict.memoryA;
if (keep.id === remove.id) throw new Error('conflict resolution would delete the kept memory');
```

The hazard is real in this codebase: `libs/memoryConflicts.ts` builds `superseded` conflicts as `memoryA: newer, memoryB: older` — the slots are semantic, not positional. The merge branch mirrors two backend refusals (either side `core`, or `persona_id` mismatch) with the comment "The backend rejects both — this just surfaces a clear message instead of a generic failure," and a merge hard-deletes BOTH originals via the atomic `merge_memories` command (`src/api/overview/memories.ts` — one SQL transaction).

`src/features/overview/sub_memories/libs/memoryConflicts.ts` — `detectConflicts` runs over every memory pair. Three layered optimizations, each documented with its math: (1) **Precompute**: "previously each memory's title+content was re-tokenized and re-bigrammed for every pair (~3·(n-1) times per memory)" — now a `MemoryFeatures[]` is built once. (2) **One pass, two metrics**: "Single intersection pass powers both word-Jaccard and topic overlap." (3) **Best-case bound pruning**:

```ts
// Zero shared tokens => topic = 0 and sim <= TEXT_SIM_BIGRAM_WEIGHT (0.6)
// < DUPLICATE_THRESHOLD (0.7): no conflict kind can fire — skip before
// touching the larger bigram sets.
if (intersection === 0 && ...) continue;
// Only pay for the bigram Jaccard when even a perfect bigram match
// could push the blended score over the duplicate threshold.
```

The bound arguments are written next to the constants they depend on (weights imported from `@/lib/memoryLimits`), so a future threshold change confronts the proof.

`src/features/overview/sub_memories/libs/memoryActions.ts`:

```ts
/** In-memory mirror of the last successful load. Acts as a session-scoped
 * backup so a mid-session corruption of localStorage[STORAGE_KEY] (truncation,
 * manual edit, quota eviction) doesn't discard the rules the user has already
 * seen in this session. `saveActions` keeps it in sync. */
let _sessionBackup: MemoryAction[] = [];
let _hasReportedCorruption = false;
```

The failure taxonomy is deliberate: storage access denied -> `silentCatch` (Sentry only, no toast) + return backup; parse failure -> `toastCatch` once per session, with copy branching on `_sessionBackup.length > 0` ("restored from this session" vs "may need to be re-created"); a non-array parse is *promoted* to corruption (`throw new Error('expected array…')`) so later callers never iterate a non-iterable. `saveActions` sets the mirror *before* attempting the write, so a quota failure "is not a data-loss bug (the in-memory backup still holds the rules)."

`src/features/overview/sub_manual-review/components/dispatch/dispatchModel.ts` — `buildDispatchRows` merges the approved-ideas page with the undispatched signal:

```ts
// The two lists are read with different limits and different filters, so they
// are NOT guaranteed to cover each other. An undispatched idea that the
// Backlog's page never loaded is exactly the row this panel exists to show —
// dropping it because the other list is short would rebuild the bug.
const seen = new Set(rows.map((r) => r.id));
for (const signal of signals ?? []) {
  if (seen.has(signal.id)) continue;
  rows.push({ id: signal.id, title: signal.title, description: '', ... });
}
```

The test file states the contract as a sentence: "What is pinned here is that the merge never invents, never drops, and never softens a blocker" (`__tests__/dispatchModel.test.ts`), with cases like *'still shows an undispatched idea the approved page never loaded'* and *'never lists the same idea twice when both sources carry it'*. Related honesty rules ride along: `ageHours: null` when a row has a task ("never 0, which would read as 'just now'") and `compareDispatch` sorts unknown ages after measured ones ("it is not evidence of urgency").

`src/features/overview/sub_manual-review/components/dispatch/DispatchPanel.tsx` — the panel reuses the one `dev_ideas` data path (`useBacklogQueue('accepted')`) while the Backlog reads the same store slice on `pending`:

```ts
// `triageItems` is ONE shared list and this panel narrows it to `accepted`.
// Whatever surface was reading it before (the Backlog, on `pending`) would
// otherwise be left filtering the wrong bucket and render empty after the
// panel closes — so the query it was using is captured on the first render
// and re-issued on unmount.
const [priorQuery] = useState(lastTriageQuery);
useEffect(() => () => {
  if (priorQuery) void fetchTriageIdeas(priorQuery.projectId, priorQuery.query);
}, [priorQuery, fetchTriageIdeas]);
```

This is the flip side of the repo's 'one data path' consolidation (`useBacklogQueue.ts` header: "same rows, two data paths, two truths" was the prior bug): once multiple surfaces share one store list, whoever re-scopes it owns restoring it. The store also exposes `lastTriageQuery` explicitly to make the restoration possible — the capability is designed in, not scraped.

`src/features/overview/sub_incidents/components/IncidentsInbox.tsx`:

```ts
// On leaving the inbox, stamp "now" as the last-seen mark so the next visit
// highlights only what arrived while away. Runs once on unmount.
useEffect(() => () => {
  localStorage.setItem(LAST_SEEN_KEY, new Date().toISOString());
}, []);
```

The derived count guards its own parse (`Number.isNaN(cutoff) -> 0`) and is null-safe on first-ever visit ("no marker shown"). The banner renders `new_since_last_visit` with a `mark_seen` button that re-stamps immediately. This costs one localStorage key and zero schema — appropriate for an ambient 'what changed' cue on a surface that already has real per-row lifecycle (open/acknowledged/resolved), where adding per-row `seen_at` would duplicate state the lifecycle doesn't need.

`src/api/devTools/workspaces.ts` — `ConsultStats` for the CLI consult layer over workspace playbooks:

```ts
/**
 * How often the CLI consult layer actually reached each playbook, plus the
 * intents it could NOT match.
 *
 * The unmatched list is the more valuable half: it is the fabric telling its
 * curator which situation has no playbook yet, sourced from real requests
 * rather than a guess.
 */
export interface ConsultStats {
  perPlaybook: { slug: string; matches: number }[];
  unmatched: { intent: string; createdAt: string }[];
}
```

The wrapper also documents its own degradation: "Degrades to a rejected promise on a binary that predates the command; every caller is expected to `silentCatch` it into an empty rail." The same telemetry-of-absence idea appears in `src/api/overview/events.ts` (`getEventSkippedStats` — the no-subscriber rate as a dead/misrouted-trigger signal).
